description:
Reorganize the growing product implementation into cohesive private bounded modules before the next adapter and persistence layers amplify coupling.

Background: the owner explicitly asked for a clean crate split. The approved distribution contract simultaneously fixes Omnirepo as one publishable binary-only product with no supported Rust library API. Cargo cannot safely ship an unpublished path dependency inside that package, and introducing published runtime libraries would silently create new public API and release surfaces. Therefore the lawful, package-safe interpretation is:

- keep the shipped product as one binary crate;
- keep src/main.rs as a thin composition root;
- organize product code into private bounded module trees with explicit dependency direction;
- retain omnirepo-dev and omnirepo-test-support as the two genuinely separate publish-false workspace crates;
- do not create a generic shared library, prelude, service locator, or catch-all utility module.

Target bounded contexts are configuration authority, managed-content planning/transactions, source snapshots/acquisition, repository state/policy, run journal/lifecycle, platform authority/adapters, and CLI composition. Effect-free domain modules sit inward; filesystem/process/network/Git adapters depend inward; application services compose domain plus ports; CLI owns parsing/rendering only. Modules must communicate through small typed contracts rather than reaching into sibling internals.

This is a structure-only convergence task. It must preserve every behavior and test, avoid a public Rust surface, and make subsequent vertical slices land in their final homes instead of accumulating unrelated top-level files.
